[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Asynchronous File Channels and Modern HTTP Client in Java"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Modern Java provides powerful concurrency and networking APIs that allow developers to build highly efficient, non-blocking I/O applications. In this tutorial, we will explore Asynchronous File Channels (java.nio.channels.AsynchronousFileChannel) and the modern HTTP Client (java.net.http.HttpClient) to perform non-blocking file operations and network requests."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding Asynchronous File Channels"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Asynchronous File Channels enable reading from and writing to files without blocking the calling thread. Instead of waiting for the I/O operation to complete, the operation is initiated, and control is returned immediately to the thread. Completion is handled using either java.util.concurrent.Future or a java.nio.channels.CompletionHandler callback."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"Path path = Paths.get("example.txt");
AsynchronousFileChannel fileChannel = AsynchronousFileChannel.open(path, StandardOpenOption.READ);
ByteBuffer buffer = ByteBuffer.allocate(1024);

fileChannel.read(buffer, 0, buffer, new CompletionHandler<Integer, ByteBuffer>() {
    @Override
    public void completed(Integer result, ByteBuffer attachment) {
        System.out.println("Bytes read: " + result);
        attachment.flip();
        // Process data here
    }

    @Override
    public void failed(Throwable exc, ByteBuffer attachment) {
        exc.printStackTrace();
    }
});"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"Introduction to the Modern HTTP Client"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"Introduced in Java 11, the java.net.http.HttpClient API replaces the legacy HttpURLConnection. It provides comprehensive support for HTTP/2, WebSockets, and asynchronous requests natively using CompletableFuture."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"HttpClient client = HttpClient.newBuilder()
    .version(HttpClient.Version.HTTP_2)
    .followRedirects(HttpClient.Redirect.NORMAL)
    .connectTimeout(Duration.ofSeconds(10))
    .build();

HttpRequest request = HttpRequest.newBuilder()
    .uri(URI.create("https://api.github.com"))
    .header("Accept", "application/json")
    .GET()
    .build();"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Combining Asynchronous File Channels with HTTP Client"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"A common use case in enterprise applications is downloading a file asynchronously over HTTP and writing it directly to disk using non-blocking file channels. The modern HTTP client supports body handlers that can stream responses directly to a file."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"Path destination = Paths.get("downloaded.zip");

CompletableFuture<Path> futureResponse = client.sendAsync(
    HttpRequest.newBuilder().uri(URI.create("https://example.com/file.zip")).build(),
    HttpResponse.BodyHandlers.ofFile(destination)
);

futureResponse.thenApply(HttpResponse::body)
    .thenAccept(path -> System.out.println("File downloaded to: " + path))
    .join();"},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always close your AsynchronousFileChannel instances to prevent resource leaks."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use HttpResponse.BodyHandlers.ofFile() for efficient streaming of large payloads directly to storage."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Leverage CompletableFuture composition methods like thenCompose() and thenAccept() to chain asynchronous operations safely."}]
